, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": -0.5, "poll_sd": 14.35, "poll_weight": 0.11, "fundamentals": {"margin": 3.15, "lean": -3.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.05}, "rating_margin": -0.25, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.7, "baseline_margin": 0.93, "analyst_adjustment": 0.07, "key_factors": ["Ten of ten expert raters call it a Tossup, with one Lean D and one Tilt R outlier", "Favorable Democratic national environment offsets the district's R+3 lean and Huizenga's incumbency", "McCann has kept pace financially with the incumbent and benefits from outside opposition spending against Huizenga", "Buttigieg rallies and heavy late attention signal Democratic momentum in Kalamazoo-area turf"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a genuine dead heat: the district's Republican lean and Huizenga's incumbency are offset by a strong Democratic environment, and the lone recent poll shows a one-point Democratic edge. Markets price the race essentially even, so a coin-flip with a slight Democratic tilt fits the evidence.", "watch": "A late-breaking independent poll or a shift in gas-price and cost-of-living headlines could break the deadlock in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Southwest Michigan's fourth district has emerged as one of the closest House races of the cycle. Incumbent Bill Huizenga, seeking another term in a district that leans Republican by three points, faces state senator Sean McCann in a contest that every major race rater now places in Tossup territory. McCann has raised nearly as much as the incumbent and benefits from outside spending attacking Huizenga, while Huizenga holds a cash advantage and the structural edge of incumbency.\n\nThe campaign's final weeks have centered on Kalamazoo, where McCann has drawn large crowds with Pete Buttigieg's help, while Huizenga contends with rising gas and diesel prices becoming a campaign issue. With no high-quality re